00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:58.620
The strategy is straightforward in concept, but requires precision and execution. You look to buy

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.140
the target after a definitive agreement has been announced, but before the shareholder vote and

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:08.540
ultimate closing. By doing so, you capture the spread between the current trading price and the

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:14.340
offer price. As long as the deal closes, time decay actually works in your favor because every day

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:20.240
that passes brings you closer to receiving the fixed cash consideration. Today's announcement for

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:26.420
Boralex illustrates the setup almost perfectly. The deal provides shareholders who tender a clean exit

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:32.060
at a fixed price, effectively removing equity market volatility and sector-specific risks for the

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:37.280
holding period. To implement this strategy in your own account, start by using your broker's screener

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.680
or a free tool like FinViz or TradingView to filter for announced all cash takeover offers that

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:48.660
carry premiums typically between 20% and 40%. That range tends to balance attractive return with

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:54.320
reasonable probability of completion. Next, note the expected closing date, most Canadian and

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.440
cross-border deals close between three and nine months after announcement. You can then calculate a

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:05.080
rough annualized spread return using the simple formula, offer price, minus current trading price,

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:10.300
divided by current trading price, divided by the number of months to expected close multiplied by

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:15.760
12. This gives you an annualized percentage that you can compare against other opportunities in your

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:22.060
pipeline. Of course, you must monitor regulatory approvals closely, competition bureau in Canada,

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:28.280
CFIUS, if there are US assets and any foreign investment reviews in Europe, pay special attention to

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:33.760
material adverse change clauses because a sharp deterioration in power prices or a major contract loss

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:39.020
could theoretically give the buyers an out. Historically, this strategy has performed best in stable

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:43.100
or falling interest rate environments when the buyers have bulletproof balance sheets.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:49.380
Risks include deal-break fees that might not fully compensate you, regulatory blocks that come out

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:55.640
of left field or more rarely, a superior competing bid that emerges late in the process. Position

